 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Authorizes the chief administrator of the courts to require and provide at least three hours of annual training regarding bail, recognizance, and commitment procedures and standards for all judges exercising preliminary jurisdiction over any offense.",
 "title": "Authorizes the chief administrator of the courts to require and provide annual training regarding bail, recognizance, and commitment procedures and standards",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A4899",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A4899-2023",
     "printNo": "A4899",
     "session": 2023,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 121\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 4899, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to author-\n      izing the chief administrator of the courts to require and provide\n      at least three hours of annual training regarding  bail,  recogni-\n      zance, and commitment procedures and standards\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would require the chief administrator of the courts to annu-\nally  require  and  provide  at  least three hours of training regarding\nbail, recognizance, and commitment  procedures  and  standards  for  all\njudges exercising preliminary jurisdiction over any offense.\n \n  It  is critically important that judges be well versed in the law, and\nespecially any updates to the law. To that end, all judges are  required\nto  attend  at least twenty-four hours of training and education courses\nevery two years. The New York State Judicial Institute  was  established\nby  law as a court-related agency to provide such judicial education and\nensure judges remain current on developments in the  law.  The  Judicial\nInstitute  is  a model for judicial education across the country and has\nalready developed and provided training regarding the laws  relating  to\nbail, recognizance and commitment procedures and standards.\n \n  The  New  York  State Constitution delineates a governmental framework\nbased on the separation of powers, wherein  the  Legislative,  Executive\nand  Judicial  branches operate independently. Therefore, determinations\nregarding education and training of  judges  should  remain  within  the\nauthority of the Judicial branch.\n \n  Recognizing  the  need for additional education around this issue, the\nOffice of Court Administration has voluntarily  committed  to  requiring\nadditional  judicial  training regarding bail, recognizance, and commit-\nment procedures for all judges exercising preliminary jurisdiction  over\ncriminal  matters.  In  the first two years of exercising such jurisdic-\ntion, judges will receive six hours of such training, and  more  experi-\nenced  judges  with  criminal  jurisdiction will receive an hour of such\ntraining each year for the next three years.  Accordingly,  this  legis-\nlation is unnecessary.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 121,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2023
   }
